  {topic:'ob',title:'OB: play again from where you were',text:'The thick red lines highlight the OB boundary. On a real course, OB is normally marked by white stakes or lines. If your ball goes beyond the boundary, the shot counts, you add one penalty stroke, and you play again from your previous spot. Here you started on the tee, so your next tee shot is your third shot.'},
  {topic:'water',title:'Water: a penalty and a drop',text:'The blue outline marks the water. Taking penalty relief adds one stroke, then you play from a drop. The drop is based on where the ball last crossed the penalty-area edge; red and yellow markings give different options. This picture shows our example’s designated drop.'},
  {topic:'bunker',title:'Sand: a harder next shot',text:`The gold outline marks the bunker. There is no automatic penalty stroke for landing in it. Think of sand as extra work on your next shot. Our Tour-player reference puts a 20 yd bunker shot about ${sand20} strokes harder than the same distance from fairway; at 60 yd, about ${sand60}. These are averages, not penalties or your personal score.`},
  {topic:'trees',title:'Rough and trees: extra work',text:'Thicker grass can make clean contact harder. Trees can block the direct route, so you may need to play a recovery shot back into space. Neither adds an automatic penalty just for landing there, but getting back on track can cost strokes.'},
  {topic:'pattern',title:'Make room for the costly miss',text:'Your shots do not all finish at one point. Think of your target as the middle of a splash of paint. Move the splash away from expensive trouble, while still leaving a useful next shot. Next, you will aim at a highlighted target and try the idea.'}
